CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ MD->MS then conversion-operator readback: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*is unknown.*$
--
[over.match.conv] with [temp.deduct]/8: an instantiated template conversion
function is not a viable *non-template* conversion-operator candidate; failing
to form its call is a soft non-viability handled by fresh template deduction,
not a hard error.

For mutually-convertible class types each carrying a templated conversion
operator, deducing a conversion (e.g. MD -> int) instantiates the source
class's `operator(signed_int)` as a class component.  That instance is not
registered in the class cpp_scope for name lookup, yet the non-template branch
of user_defined_conversion_sequence iterated it and built a name-driven member
call, which threw "symbol 'operator(signed_int)' is unknown" and aborted the
enclosing statement (silently dropping the assertion -> vacuous SUCCESS).

The non-template branch now forms that member call inside a SFINAE context and
treats a resolution failure as a soft skip, leaving the candidate to the fresh
template-conversion deduction.  Minimal model of libstdc++'s
std::ranges::__detail::__max_size_type / __max_diff_type.

